  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 328 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nAnother pleasant fall day is in store today with light winds \nand slightly warmer temperatures in the low to mid 60s.\nNorthwest winds will keep temperatures cooler near the \nlakeshore in Indiana, in the mid to upper 50s. Only item of \nnote for tonight is a low chance for fog development near and \nsouth of I-80. Have held off on a formal mention in the official\nforecast for now due to the potential for high clouds to move \nin earlier and also noting that hi-res guidance has been over- \nforecasting fog development the past few days.\n\nHeading into Wednesday, a compact shortwave and associated \nsurface low are expected to track across the region. There \nremains some uncertainty in the coverage of showers owing to \nresidual low-level dry air, which may keep things more isolated\nto scattered initially. Have accordingly maintained mainly 30 \nto 50 percent chances for showers with this update. Steepening \nmid-level lapse rates may provide just enough instability for \nincreasing shower coverage in the afternoon along with the \npotential for lightning strikes, mainly south of a Pontiac to \nRensselaer line (20% chance).\n\nThe departing wave eventually phases with an amplifying upper\ntrough across the eastern CONUS Wednesday night, which will \nallow a much cooler airmass to descend into the region through \nthe end of the week. While embedded mid-level vort lobes \nrounding the resultant closed upper low may bring a few showers \nfarther inland through the day on Thursday, focus continues to \nbe on the persistent signal for a robust band of lake effect \nshowers to set up over Lake Michigan, potentially through early \nSaturday morning. This continues to seem reasonable at this \ndistance given favorable lake-induced thermodynamics and \nnortherly fetch. While occasional westward wobbles in the \nposition of the band may occur, trends have been toward the band\nfocusing mainly into northwest Indiana Thursday night through \nFriday.\n\nThe primary precipitation type is expected to by rain with \nthese showers, though snow is likely to mix in at times. There \nhas been ensemble support for slushy accumulations into portions\nof northwest Indiana. However, warm lake temperatures (mid \n50s), marginal convective cloud-top temperatures (~11C), \ncombined with warm antecedent ground temperatures may limit this\npotential overall. With all of this said, we will continue to \nmonitor trends closely. Winds will also be strong near the \nlakeshore in Indiana with gusts up to 35-40 mph at times during \nthis period.\n\nIn addition to cooler daytime temperatures, inland areas away \nfrom the lake effect clouds may see temperatures fall near to \nbelow freezing each night through the end of the week. Long- \nrange ensemble guidance indicates the colder pattern will be \nfairly short-lived as upper ridging builds back into the region\nearly next week, bringing a return to above normal temperatures\nfor mid-November.\n\nPetr",
